ABSTRACT: The digitization of cultural heritage, while expanding its accessibility and scientific value, increases its vulnerability to increasingly sophisticated cyber threats, with potential impacts on preservation, integrity and public trust. Cultural institutions, similar in size and operations to Small and Medium Enterprises (SMEs), face increasing cybersecurity challenges, hampered by limited resources, lack of expertise, and regulatory complexities. This article presents PACY (Pro-Active Cybersecurity), an innovative Artificial Intelligence-based platform designed to simplify cybersecurity management for such entities. PACY leverages Large Language Models (LLMs) through a specialized multi-Agent architecture, in which dedicated AI agents, refined through prompt engineering, guide non-expert users through diagnosis and incident response. By natively integrating Open Source Intelligence (OSINT) sources such as VirusTotal and IntelX, and offering natural language interaction, PACY simplifies threat analysis. A distinguishing feature is the automatic generation of structured incident reports, essential for compliance and escalation. Initial validation shows how PACY significantly reduces cognitive load and improves operational effectiveness compared to generalist LLMs, bridging the skills gap for cultural institutions. © 2025 IEEE.

[R0061] year=2025 | venue=2025 8th International Conference on Circuit, Power and Computing Technologies, ICCPCT 2025 | type=Conference paper
TITLE: Intelligent Dynamic Code Analysis using Large Language Models
KEYWORDS: Automated Vulnerability Detection; Dynamic Code Analysis; Intelligent Debugging; Large Language Models (LLMs); Software Security; Codes (symbols); Dynamic analysis; Learning algorithms; Learning systems; Machine learning; Network security; Security systems; Automated vulnerability detection; Code analysis; Dynamic code analyze; Intelligent debugging; Language model; Large language model; Performance metrices; Security assessment; Software security; Vulnerability detection; Software design
ABSTRACT: This paper presents an AI-driven approach to enhance dynamic code analysis by leveraging Large Language Models (LLMs), such as Falcon 40 B and Code Llama. The proposed framework continuously monitors key performance metrics-execution time, memory utilization, and computational overhead-while employing machine learning algorithms to detect inefficiencies and security vulnerabilities in real-time. Unlike traditional dynamic analysis methods, which rely on predefined rules, this system adapts to emerging threats and evolving vulnerabilities, streamlining code review and security assessments. By analyzing runtime data and recognizing patterns, the LLMs provide actionable recommendations to improve both performance and security. Experimental results demonstrate that this AI-assisted approach significantly improves security posture, reduces manual intervention, and enhances overall code maintainability. Despite challenges such as false positives and ethical concerns, the proposed method offers a scalable, efficient solution for secure software development. Future work will focus on refining model transparency, minimizing biases, and integrating AI-driven security assessments with penetration testing workflows. © 2025 IEEE.

[R0062] year=2025 | venue=Proceedings of the Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics | type=Conference paper
TITLE: Red-Teaming LLM Multi-Agent Systems via Communication Attacks
KEYWORDS: Computational linguistics; Computational methods; Intelligent agents; Network security; Problem solving; Agent collaboration; Agents coordination; Communication framework; Complex problem solving; Language model; Message-based communication; Model-based OPC; Multiagent systems (MASs); Red teaming; Security vulnerabilities; Multi agent systems
ABSTRACT: Large Language Model-based Multi-Agent Systems (LLM-MAS) have revolutionized complex problem-solving capability by enabling sophisticated agent collaboration through message-based communications. While the communication framework is crucial for agent coordination, it also introduces a critical yet unexplored security vulnerability. In this work, we introduce Agent-in-the-Middle (AiTM), a novel attack that exploits the fundamental communication mechanisms in LLM-MAS by intercepting and manipulating inter-agent messages. Unlike existing attacks that compromise individual agents, AiTM demonstrates how an adversary can compromise entire multi-agent systems by only manipulating the messages passing between agents. To enable the attack under the challenges of limited control and role-restricted communication format, we develop an LLM-powered adversarial agent with a reflection mechanism that generates contextually-aware malicious instructions. Our comprehensive evaluation across various frameworks, communication structures, and real-world applications demonstrates that LLM-MAS is vulnerable to communication-based attacks, highlighting the need for robust security measures in multi-agent systems. © 2025 Association for Computational Linguistics.

