ABSTRACT: Continuous delivery pipelines that leverage large language models (LLMs) to author code, tests, and infrastructure manifest offload human toil but amplify the blast radius of defects when pipeline stages fail. We propose Zero Touch GenAI Coach (ZTC), an autonomous, reinforcement learning agent that monitors CI/CD telemetry, diagnoses failures, and submits self-healing pull requests in less than two minutes without human intervention. ZTC ingests build logs, static + dynamic traces, and GenAI prompt context, then uses a policy guard railed LLM to generate remediation diffs. Deployed across 320 micro services at three U.S. fintech startups, ZTC reduced mean time to recovery (MTTR) by 37%, cut pipeline failure rate by 29%, and boosted developer satisfaction by 18%. We open source the agent framework, RL reward model, and an anonymized 50 k failure dataset under Apache 2.0. © The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Singapore Pte Ltd. 2026.

[R0068] year=2026 | venue=IEEE Access | type=Article
TITLE: SYNAPSE: Synthetic Network-Traffic Agent Pipeline for Federated Edge Security
KEYWORDS: anomaly detection; edge network security; federated edge computing; Federated learning; network intrusion detection; network traffic generation; synthetic network traffic; Differential privacy; Edge computing; Edge detection; Internet protocols; Intrusion detection; Network intrusion; Network security; Privacy-preserving techniques; Telecommunication traffic; Anomaly detection; Edge computing; Edge network security; EDGE Networks; Federated edge computing; Network intrusion detection; Network traffic; Network traffic generation; Networks security; Synthetic network traffic; Synthetic networks; Anomaly detection; Budget control
ABSTRACT: Ensuring federated edge networks’ security from intrusion attacks creates a complex problem in itself, wherein distributed nodes need to be provided with vast amounts of diverse traffic, which is stratified by protocols and needs labeling, and yet real traffic is limited due to its legal complications. Existing synthetic traffic generators address this gap inadequately. Statistical generators reproduce marginal feature distributions but discard the causal network behaviors that make attacks detectable. Static-template generators produce causally valid traffic but collapse to narrow regions of the feature space, causing federated models to overfit to fixed tool parameters rather than learning generalizable detection boundaries. Neither class of generator addresses the schema mismatches, non-independent and identically distributed partitioning requirements, or threshold calibration mismatches that arise specifically in federated deployment. This paper introduces SYNAPSE, a Synthetic Network-Traffic Agent Pipeline for federated Edge Security. SYNAPSE coordinates four tightly coupled stages: an agentic synthetic generation engine driven by a large language model operating within a Planner-Configurer-Executor-Reflector state machine over live Containernet network emulation; a deterministic schema adapter that bridges the 82-dimensional CIC-IDS2017 feature space to the 66-dimensional protocol-partitioned detector input; a federated detection mesh of four edge nodes aggregated via FedProx with trust-weighted aggregation under Dirichlet-partitioned non-independent and identically distributed data; and a privacy-preserving intelligence layer combining central differential privacy noise injected at the FedProx aggregator with an agentic retrieval-augmented generation reporting module. Across volumetric attack scenarios, SYNAPSE achieves an intrusion detection rate of 99.5 percent and a five-class detection F1 of 70.0 percent (accuracy 70.4 percent), while reducing the false positive rate by 18.3 percentage points relative to a static-template baseline. Federated convergence reaches 98.4 percent global accuracy within the target round budget, and the privacy-preserving layer retains 96.8 percent of detection F1 under a formal differential privacy guarantee. © 2013 IEEE.

[R0069] year=2025 | venue=21st International Wireless Communications and Mobile Computing Conference, IWCMC 2025 | type=Conference paper
TITLE: Autonomous Cyber Incident Response Using Reasoning and Action
KEYWORDS: Automated Incident Response; Autonomous Cyber Defense; Large Language Models; Real-Time Threat Mitigation; Reasoning and Acting Agents; Autonomous agents; Cybersecurity; Malware; Network security; Automated incident response; Autonomous cybe defense; Cyber-defense; Incident response; Language model; Large language model; Real- time; Real-time threat mitigation; Reasoning and acting agent; Threats mitigations; Decision making
ABSTRACT: The increasing complexity and frequency of cyber threats necessitate autonomous security solutions capable of real-time detection, reasoning, and response. This paper introduces an autonomous cyber incident response framework that integrates Reasoning and Acting (ReAct) agents with Large Language Models (LLMs) to enhance cybersecurity decision-making. The proposed system features a cloud-based testbed, real-time monitoring tools (Wazuh, Suricata), and a NATS messaging system for seamless threat detection and mitigation. The ReAct agent, powered by a fine-tuned LLM, iteratively analyzes security alerts, generates context-aware mitigation strategies, and autonomously executes response actions via integrated cybersecurity tools such as firewalls. The framework demonstrates its effectiveness in real-time cyberattack mitigation, including port scanning, botnet intrusions, and SSH brute-force attacks. By leveraging LangGraph-guided decision loops and Chain-of-Thought (CoT) reasoning, the system dynamically adapts to evolving threats while reducing reliance on human intervention. Evaluations in a simulated environment highlight the scalability of the architecture and its ability to achieve low-latency, autonomous threat mitigation. The findings highlight the potential of LLM-driven security automation in modern cyber defense strategies, paving the way for future advancements in mitigating phishing, malware, and insider threats. © 2025 IEEE.

[R0070] year=2025 | venue=2025 22nd Annual International Conference on Privacy, Security, and Trust, PST 2025 | type=Conference paper
TITLE: RefPentester: A Knowledge-Informed Self-Reflective Penetration Testing Framework Based on Large Language Models
KEYWORDS: Automated Penetration Testing; Large Language Model; PT Stage Machine; Reflexion; Retrieval Augmented Generation; Computational linguistics; Automated penetration testing; Language model; Large language model; Model-based OPC; Penetration testing; PT stage machine; Reflexion; Retrieval augmented generation; Target systems; Testing framework; Ability testing
ABSTRACT: Automated penetration testing (AutoPT) powered by large language models (LLMs) has gained attention for its ability to automate ethical hacking processes and identify vulnerabilities in target systems by leveraging the inherent knowledge of LLMs. However, existing LLM-based AutoPT frameworks often underperform compared to human experts in challenging tasks for several reasons: the imbalanced knowledge used in LLM training, short-sightedness in the planning process, and hallucinations during command generation. Moreover, the trial-and-error nature of the PT process is constrained by existing frameworks lacking mechanisms to learn from previous failures, restricting adaptive improvement of PT strategies. To address these limitations, we propose a knowledge-informed, selfreflective PT framework powered by LLMs, called RefPentester. This AutoPT framework is designed to assist human operators in identifying the current stage of the PT process, selecting appropriate tactics and techniques for each stage, choosing suggested actions, providing step-by-step operational guidance, and reflecting on and learning from previous failed operations. We also modeled the PT process as a seven-state Stage Machine to integrate the proposed framework effectively. The evaluation shows that RefPentester can successfully reveal credentials on Hack The Box's Sau machine, outperforming the baseline GPT40 model by 16.7%. Across PT stages, RefPentester also demonstrates superior success rates on PT stage transitions. © 2025 IEEE.

[R0071] year=2026 | venue=2026 7th International Conference on Information Technology and Education Technology, ITET 2026 | type=Conference paper
TITLE: Teaching Graduate Level Ethical Hacking in the Era of Large Language Models - A Case Study
KEYWORDS: cybersecurity; education; ethical hacking; LLMs; penetration testing; Curricula; Cybersecurity; Education computing; Engineering education; Ethical technology; Software engineering; Students; Teaching; Atypicals; Best practices; Case-studies; Cyber security; Ethical hacking; Graduate-level; Language model; Large language model; Penetration testing; University levels; Personal computing
ABSTRACT: Teaching university-level ethical hacking is nontrivial due to an atypical pedagogy in comparison with software engineering or computer science best practices, the breadth of knowledge required for students and the requirement for a secure laboratory. The rapid adoption of Large Language Models (LLMs) further complicates student assessment, requiring novel assessment methods. In this paper, we present the course Fundamental of ethical hacking, a graduate level course designed to teach the ethical hacking methodology, develop confidence and skills in practical attack methods and provide a baseline for future and continuous education. We present the course curriculum, recently adapted to accommodate student use of LLMs. We then present the student evaluations of the course and validate the changes made via a study on student use of LLMs. We conclude with future research directions in the use of LLMs for ethical hacking education. © 2026 IEEE.

[R0072] year=2026 | venue=IEEE Geoscience and Remote Sensing Letters | type=Article
TITLE: Securing Multiagent GIS Systems: Risk Evaluation and Prompt Hardening Optimization
