TITLE: IoT-CyberAgent: An Intelligent Multi-Agent Cyber Threat Detection and Response System for IoT Networks
KEYWORDS: Cyberattack Mitigation; Ensemble Learning; Intrusion Detection System; IoT Traffic Analysis; Large Language Models; Retrieval-Augmented Generation; Intelligent agents; Internet of things; Learning systems; Multi agent systems; Recommender systems; Cyber-attacks; Cyberattack mitigation; Ensemble learning; Internet of thing traffic analyze; Intrusion Detection Systems; Language model; Large language model; Real- time; Retrieval-augmented generation; Traffic analysis; Intrusion detection
ABSTRACT: The Internet of Things (IoT) has provided real-time connectivity across smart environments but has also been vulnerable to cyberattacks. We propose IoT-CyberAgent, a novel real-time multi-agent IoT threat mitigation and recommendation system that integrates large language models (LLMs) with machine learning techniques. IoT-CyberAgent performs real-time feature engineering to classify specific IoT attacks, and the recommendation agent provides mitigation strategies through contextual searches. Experimental results demonstrate improved detection accuracy, real-time adaptability, and effective recommendation solutions. This system addresses the need for better attack detection and response in IoT environments by combining classical ML models and LLMs for deeper understanding and contextual recommendations. © 2026 IEEE.

[R0075] year=2026 | venue=Electronics (Switzerland) | type=Article
TITLE: H2-MAS: A Hybrid Heuristic-Multi-Agent Architecture for Semantic Bot Detection
KEYWORDS: adversarial defense; bot detection; large language models; multi-agent systems; semantic security; web application firewall; Architecture; Behavioral research; Bot (Internet); Botnet; Computer system firewalls; Cost effectiveness; Intelligent agents; Network security; User experience; Adversarial defense; Bot detections; Hybrid heuristics; Language model; Large language model; Multiagent systems (MASs); Power users; Semantic security; Theoretical capacity; Web application firewalls; Multi agent systems
ABSTRACT: Traditional Web Application Firewalls (WAFs) rely on static thresholds to detect automated threats. While effective against simple scripts, these deterministic rules struggle with “Ambiguous Traffic”—sophisticated bots that mimic human behavior and “Efficient Humans” (Power Users) who exhibit bot-like speed. In this paper, we introduce H2-MAS, a hierarchical security framework that combines a high-speed Random Forest classifier (Tier 1) with a Multi-Agent Cognitive Council (Tier 2) powered by Large Language Models (LLMs). Unlike standard “Black Box” LLM deployments, H2-MAS utilizes an adversarial “Prosecutor vs. Defender” protocol to resolve semantic paradoxes in real-time. We evaluated the system on a stratified dataset of 1000 high-uncertainty sessions ( (Formula presented.) ). Through simulated evaluation on a stratified semantic test set, the results demonstrate that the proposed architecture has the theoretical capacity to achieve up to 97.6% accuracy on specific edge cases where traditional heuristics fail. Notably, the Cognitive Council demonstrated the theoretical capacity to reduce the False Positive Rate to 0.00% within this constrained evaluated set, validating that the “Defender” agent can successfully protect legitimate power users from erroneous blocking. This architecture offers a cost-effective paradigm for Semantic Security, prioritizing user experience without compromising threat detection. © 2026 by the author.

[R0076] year=2025 | venue=Procedia Computer Science | type=Conference paper
TITLE: Generative AI in minimizing cyber-attacks: Developing the Vehicular Threat Intelligence Flowchart
KEYWORDS: Cyber attacks; Cybersecurity; GenAI; Threat Detection; Vehicular Networks; Autonomous vehicles; Autonomous Vehicles; Cyber security; Cyber-attacks; Generative artificial intelligence; Intelligent transport; Public safety; Threat detection; Transport systems; Vehicle control system; Vehicular networks; Cyber attacks
ABSTRACT: This paper delves into the innovative applications of Generative Artificial Intelligence (GenAI) in enhancing the cybersecurity of vehicular networks, a critical area given the increasing integration of intelligent transport systems and autonomous vehicles. As vehicular networks become more sophisticated, they also become more susceptible to cyber-attacks that can compromise vehicle control systems, endangering public safety and personal privacy. GenAI offers advanced capabilities for automating defences, improving threat intelligence, and creating dynamic security frameworks that can adapt to emerging threats. This research is a comprehensive overview of the current state of GenAI in the context of vehicular network cybersecurity, highlighting the development and implementation of the Vehicular Threat Intelligence Flowchart (VTIF). The VTIF features a threat detection rule algorithm that automates the identification of cyber threats, significantly improving detection accuracy. While the integration of GenAI presents substantial benefits, it also introduces new risks, necessitating robust ethical, legal, and technical oversight. This paper outlines the potential advantages and challenges of employing GenAI in vehicular cybersecurity and proposes future research directions aimed at developing resilient and ethical cybersecurity mechanisms. © 2025 Elsevier B.V.. All rights reserved.

[R0077] year=2026 | venue=IEEE Transactions on Network Science and Engineering | type=Article
TITLE: GenTI: Benchmarking LLMs for Autonomous IDPS Rule Generation for Unseen Attacks
KEYWORDS: CTI; cybersecurity automation; IDPS; large language models (LLMs); unseen attacks; Automation; Benchmarking; Classification (of information); Cybersecurity; Intrusion detection; Large datasets; Network security; Semantics; Attack detection; Cybe threat intelligence; Cyber security; Cyber threats; Cybersecurity automation; Intrusion detection and prevention systems; Language model; Large language model; Rule generation; Unseen attack; Syntactics
ABSTRACT: Rule-based Intrusion Detection and Prevention Systems (IDPS) offer precise attack detection as well as mitigation, however their manually crafted, signature-driven rules limit adaptability to emerging and zero-day threats. Additionally, existing public datasets (e.g., CICIDS2017, UNSW-NB15) focus on traffic classification and provide little structured information to support automatic rule synthesis or prevention logic. To address this gap, we propose Generative Thread Intelligence (GenTI)1 an LLM-driven benchmark for automatic generation of IDPS rules targeting unseen attacks. The dataset (GTI) aggregates over 150 k detection and prevention rules from Snort, Suricata, Emerging Threats, as well as 50 k YARA, each annotated with protocol behavior, payload signatures, contextual relationships, mappings to Cyber Threat Intelligence (CTI), along with actionable response types (alert, drop, reject). Moreover, on top of this corpus we design an LLM-based pipeline that transforms analyst prompts and representative payloads into deployable rules via structured prompt engineering, Chain-of-Thought (CoT) reasoning, as well as a Chain-of-Verification (CoVe) loop for syntactic, semantic, and security validation. The generated rules are executed in real time on (Snort/Suricata) and evaluated by syntax accuracy, semantic similarity, CTI coverage, security effectiveness as well as unseen attacks detection. Furthermore, our GenTI instantiation achieves a composite rule-quality score of 89.4%, with 94.8% CTI coverage, improving unseen attacks detection from 45% to 87.4% and reducing the false-positive rate from 8.5% to 2.3%. Overall, GenTI establishes the first large-scale benchmark that tightly couples rule-level CTI with LLM-based automation, enabling adaptive, adaptive IDPS rule generation. © 2026 IEEE. All rights reserved.

[R0078] year=2026 | venue=2026 5th International Conference on Electronics Technology and Artificial Intelligence, ETAI 2026 | type=Conference paper
TITLE: MRE-Guard: A RAG-Based Multi-Agent Framework for Phishing Detection
KEYWORDS: Email Security; Large Language Models; Multi-Agent System; Phishing Detection; RAG; Behavioral research; Computer crime; Distributed computer systems; Electronic mail filters; Information retrieval; Intelligent agents; Network security; Phishing; Contextual understanding; E-mails security; Language model; Large language model; Multiagent framework; Multiagent systems (MASs); Phishing; Phishing detections; RAG; Social engineering; Multi agent systems
ABSTRACT: To counter sophisticated social engineering attacks, traditional email filters often fail to detect novel phishing threats due to insufficient contextual understanding. This paper proposes MRE-Guard, a dual-agent collaborative detection framework. It introduces an interaction mechanism between a Retrieval Agent, which provides reliable external knowledge via a balanced retrieval strategy, and a QA Agent, which performs final decisionmaking through chain-of-thought reasoning. Experiments on a self-constructed comprehensive email dataset show that MREGuard, powered by DeepSeek, achieves an F1-score of 88.89% and an accuracy of 89.17%, outperforming zero-shot baselines by 26.49%. The results demonstrate that the synergy between retrieval and reasoning significantly enhances threat detection and interpretability in dynamic email environments. © 2026 IEEE.

[R0079] year=2026 | venue=2026 Innovations in Machine, Engineering, and Digital Conference, IMED 2026 | type=Conference paper
TITLE: Ethical Hacking in the Age of Autonomous AI: A Governance-Centric Approach to Cyber Defense
KEYWORDS: Autonomous AI; Ethical Hacking; Explainable AI; Governance-as-Code; Red Teaming; Artificial intelligence; Blockchain; Cybersecurity; Ethical technology; Network security; AI systems; Algorithmics; Autonomous AI; Cyber security; Cyber-defense; Ethical hacking; Explainable AI; Governance-as-code; Human's intuition; Red teaming; Personal computing
ABSTRACT: Autonomous and generative AI systems have turned cybersecurity into an adaptive environment in which attackers and defenders alike are becoming more algorithmic. Classical ethical hacking, which used to be based on human intuition and manual exploit trials, sees today's artificially intelligent opponents able to learn and adapt in real time. In this paper, we introduce a Governance-Centric Ethical Hacking (GCEH) framework that bakes algorithmic accountability, explainability, and blockchain-enabled auditability into each phase of AI-driven penetration testing. Through governance encoded as code, the model allows red-team efforts to respect ethical and regulatory limits without sacrificing operational flexibility. Experiments conducted in simulated enterprise testbeds show that governance-integrated AI agents enhanced the vulnerability coverage by 40%, achieved a GCI value of 0.94, and elevated ETS from 0.62 to 0.76. Moreover, the number of non-compliance violations reduced by 70%, and the ability to reproduce across runs was 95% reproducible. These findings validate that integrating dynamic policy logic inside AI operations converts cybersecurity from a reactive defense to a proactive, explainable, and ethical discipline. The GCEH model then reframes the ethical hacking activity as reproducible, auditable, and governance-aligned responsible autonomy. © 2026 IEEE.

[R0080] year=2025 | venue=Proceedings - IEEE 10th International Conference on Smart Structures and Systems, ICSSS 2025 | type=Conference paper
TITLE: Cybersecurity Threat Analysis utilizing Deep Learning: Apply Natural Language Processing Techniques to Extract Compromise Indicators from Unstructured Logs
KEYWORDS: Anomaly Detection; Indicators of Compromise; Threat Analysis; Transformer Models; Unstructured Logs; Computer crime; Cybersecurity; Data streams; Deep learning; Learning systems; Natural language processing systems; Network security; Security systems; Anomaly detection; Cyber security; Indicator of compromize; Language processing; Language processing techniques; Natural languages; Threats analysis; Transformer modeling; Unstructured data; Unstructured log; Anomaly detection
ABSTRACT: Abstract-The methods that hackers use to break into systems are becoming increasingly sophisticated. Cyber security systems of today are required to deal with a large amount of unstructured data, which includes system logs, network traffic, and threat intelligence reports, in order to find possible indicators of compromise (IoCs). Because of the many, complicated, and confusing cyber incidents that have happened in the last few years, traditional rule-based systems and manual log analysis techniques are no longer enough. This study aims to illustrate the application of an advanced deep learning architecture and Natural Language Processing (NLP) techniques to autonomously identify, interpret, and correlate indicators of compromise within unstructured cybersecurity data. The method uses transformer-based designs like BERT and GPT to figure out what words imply based on the context in which they are used. Because of this, the system can find behaviors that weren't expected, threat patterns that were hidden, and attack signatures that had never been seen before. The recommended solution uses preprocessing pipelines to do both log normalization and entity recognition. Then, it uses semantic embedding to turn unstructured log entries into vectors that the learning model can understand. It is possible for the model to detect critical signals such as IP anomalies, unlawful access attempts, and hazardous payloads without the requirement to be configured with specific criteria. The labeled threat datasets not only contribute to the further improvement of the deep learning module, but it also makes use of attention techniques in order to select the most significant tokens associated with threats. The results of experimental assessments show that the accuracy and recall of IoC extraction are significantly higher than those of typical machine learning benchmarks. Additionally, it is able to manage a wide variety of log formats and an everevolving threat vocabulary. The goal of this project is to construct an autonomous threat analysis system that is able to learn from data streams that are constantly changing. This project links natural language processing (NLP) and cybersecurity. Furthermore, it improves the effectiveness of proactive security methods and makes it simpler for analysts to identify items in real time. According to the findings of our research, powerful natural language processing (NLP)driven cybersecurity analytics have the potential to transform threat intelligence by transforming unstructured data into a dependable and efficient method of identifying and resolving new cyber threats. © 2025 IEEE.

[R0081] year=2025 | venue=Sustainable Cities and Society | type=Article
TITLE: Generative AI of things for sustainable smart cities: Synergizing cognitive augmentation, resource efficiency, network traffic, cybersecurity, and anomaly detection for environmental performance
KEYWORDS: Anomaly detection; Cognitive augmentation; Cybersecurity; Environmental sustainability; Generative artificial intelligence; Generative artificial intelligence of things; Generative internet of things; Network traffic; Resource efficiency; Sustainable smart cities; Behavioral research; Cybersecurity; Data communication systems; Data privacy; Decision making; Ecosystems; Energy efficiency; Environmental technology; Generative adversarial networks; Internet of things; Network architecture; Smart city; Sustainable city; Sustainable development; Telecommunication traffic; Urban growth; Anomaly detection; Cognitive augmentation; Cyber security; Environmental sustainability; Generative artificial intelligence; Generative artificial intelligence of thing; Generative internet of thing; Network traffic; Resource efficiencies; Sustainable smart city; artificial intelligence; cognition; decision making; smart city; sustainability; urban area; urban development; Anomaly detection
ABSTRACT: Artificial Intelligence of Things (AIoT) has emerged as a transformative technology driving environmental sustainability in smart city development. However, the integration of Generative Artificial Intelligence (GenAI) within AIoT ecosystems remains largely unexplored. Current research predominantly addresses conventional AIoT frameworks, overlooking the innovative potential of generative models, such as Generative Adversarial Networks, Variational Autoencoders, Diffusion Models, Transformers, and hybrid architectures, to significantly enhance situational awareness, system optimization, operational robustness, real-time responsiveness, and adaptive decision-making in complex urban environments. AIoT systems continue to face persistent challenges, including data scarcity, poor data quality, limited adaptability, imbalanced datasets, and inadequate context-awareness. This study addresses these gaps by systematically exploring how GenAI can enhance AIoT functionalities across key domains—namely cognitive augmentation, resource efficiency, network traffic, cybersecurity, and anomaly detection—while examining their synergistic potential to improve system-level environmental performance across two interconnected layers in sustainable smart cities. At the operational layer, key findings reveal that integrating GenAI with AIoT systems enhances urban efficiency, adaptability, autonomy, robustness, and resilience by conserving resources, optimizing network traffic flows, securing infrastructures, and detecting anomalies before they escalate. Specifically, the fusion of generative intelligence with federated learning promotes sustainable, energy-efficient AIoT deployments by reducing data transmission, thereby lowering communication overhead and safeguarding user privacy. In networked environments, generative models improve synthetic traffic realism and communication efficiency. They also strengthen cybersecurity through enhanced intrusion prevention and threat detection. Additionally, they enable early identification and mitigation of anomalies, boosting operational efficiency and system robustness. These improvements stabilize sustainable smart city system functioning and prevent disruptive failures. At the environmental layer, as key findings indicate, these operational gains cascade into indirect but tangible ecological benefits, while generative models advance the core pillars of AIoT by enabling proactive, autonomous, context-aware, and self-adaptive systems that further enhance the environmental performance of sustainable smart cities. Thus, while the five domains primarily underpin the operational backbone of urban systems, their cascading effects extend to ecological outcomes. The proposed conceptual framework, distilled from key findings, integrates GenAI and AIoT and highlights both domain-specific advancements and their synergistic interactions. This framework holds significant potential to drive sustainable smart city development by fostering AIoT ecosystems that are more intelligent, resource-efficient, adaptive, secure, robust, and autonomous through the strategic application of generative intelligence. The insights gained from this study provide policymakers, urban planners, system designers, and technology developers with practical guidance to harness GAIoT for enhancing smart city resilience, sustainability, and operational intelligence. © 2025 The Author(s)

[R0082] year=2026 | venue=Proceedings of the International Conference on Software Engineering and Knowledge Engineering, SEKE | type=Conference paper
TITLE: SAMSARA: Architecture-aware LLM-Agentic Vulnerability Detection for Microservices
KEYWORDS: cross-service vulnerabilities; large language models; microservices security; multiagent systems; SAST; vulnerability detection; Benchmarking; Codes (symbols); Computational methods; Digital storage; Iterative methods; Network security; Cross-service vulnerability; Down-stream; Language model; Large language model; Microservice security; Multi agent; SAST; Security failure; Service maps; Vulnerability detection; Multi agent systems
ABSTRACT: Many security failures in microservice systems arise not from bugs within a single service but from mismatches across services: authorization enforced upstream but skipped downstream, data crossing a service boundary without re-validation, or services with incompatible assumptions about shared storage. Pattern-based SAST analyzes code in isolation and misses these failures; prior LLM detectors focus on single projects, and static cross-service analyzers do not reason about authorization intent. This paper introduces SAMSARA, a framework that models a target codebase as a microservice system M = (S,E,D) of services, communication edges, and shared dependencies, and uses LLM agents augmented with domain-specific knowledge to detect cross-service vulnerabilities including BOLA, BFLA, and cross-service taint flows. SAMSARA runs in three phases: service-map construction, orchestrator-worker analysis with parallel Verification Agents followed by an independent Judge Agent, and iterative remediation. We implement and evaluate SAMSARA on six benchmarks: its recall margin over Semgrep is small on single-file tests (+5.8%) but large on cross-service systems (+61-85%). Component ablations show the service map drives precision (removal nearly doubles reported findings), iteration improves recall and precision, and skills shift coverage. © 2026 Knowledge Systems Institute Graduate School. All rights reserved.

[R0083] year=2025 | venue=2025 International Conference on Smart and Sustainable Technology, INCSST 2025 | type=Conference paper
TITLE: Assessment of BERT and Lightweight LLMs for Intrusion Detection in Internet of Vehicles
KEYWORDS: BERT and Lightweight LLMs; CAN bus; Intrusion Detection System; IoV; Transformer models; Buses; Computer crime; Control system synthesis; Controllers; Intrusion detection; Transformer protection; Vehicle detection; BERT and lightweight large language model; Controller-area network; Controller-area-network bus; Cyber-attacks; Internet of vehicle; Intrusion Detection Systems; Intrusion-Detection; Language model; Transformer modeling; Vehicular systems; Economic and social effects
ABSTRACT: Securing communication within vehicular systems is becoming more and more crucial as smart technologies are added to them. Despite its efficiency, the Controller Area Network (CAN) bus is vulnerable to cyberattacks due to its inherent lack of protection. Intelligent intrusion detection systems are desperately needed as autonomous and connected vehicles continue to improve. The goal of this research is to create sophisticated transformer designs that can comprehend semantic and sequence information included in CAN communication. Modern vehicular systems are becoming increasingly intelligent with the advent of the Internet of Vehicles (IoV) but they are also more vulnerable to cyberattacks, especially through the CAN bus. Conventional intrusion detection systems (IDS) frequently miss contextually relevant material and serial dependencies in CAN messages. This paper, explore the use of transformer-built large language models (LLMs) for CAN-bus intrusion detection. Using the Car Hacking dataset, we experimentally compared four transformer models: BERT, TinyBERT, AlBERT, and ELECTRA. Each model was refined using different data sets, ranging from 10,000 to 40,000 cases per class and raw CAN messages were transformed into byte sentence representations to maintain semantic structure. According to this research, TinyBERT achieved BERT-level performance while drastically lowering complexity, while ALBERT and ELECTRA models all performed accurately across a range of dataset sizes. These findings show that tiny transformers are feasible for in-vehicle, low-resource, and time-sensitive IDS. The study offers a solid standard for later transformer-based IDS in the IoV context by shedding light on model scalability, performance trade-offs and embedded applicability. © 2025 IEEE.

[R0084] year=2026 | venue=AIP Conference Proceedings | type=Conference paper
TITLE: Weaponizing language models for cybersecurity offensive operations: Automating vulnerability assessment report validation; A review paper
KEYWORDS: and Penetration testing; Cyber warfare; Large Language Models (LLMs); Machine learning; Offensive cybersecurity; Vulnerability assessment automation
ABSTRACT: This, with the ever-increasing sophistication of cyberwar, calls for novel solutions. In this regard, Large Language Models (LLMs) have emerged as a highly promising tool for defensive and offensive cybersecurity-related strategies. While existing literature has focused much on the defensive use of LLMs, when it comes to their offensive utilization, very little has been reported-namely, concerning Vulnerability Assessment (VA) report validation. Consequentially, this paper tries to fill that gap by investigating the capabilities of LLMs in automating and improving the validation process of the report of the VA. From the critical review of the related literature, this paper hereby proposes a new approach to using the LLMs in the automation of the analysis and within the validation process of the report of the VA that could potentially reduce the number of false positives and generally enhance efficiency. These results are promising for LLM automatization for improving validation on reports coming from VA in order to improve accuracy while reducing human effort and security postures. The contribution of this paper provides further evidence about the offensive and defensive LLM capabilities and therefor helps in devising more appropriate cybersecurity strategies and tools accordingly. © 2026 Author(s).


